The Interpreter/Python Numbered Parameters API
----------------------------------------------

The existing situation with numbered parameters
-----------------------------------------------

As up to now, numbered parameters were handled as an array internal to
the interpreter; and at certain points, parts of this array were
written to the 'emc.var' file. Also, at startup the 'emc.var' file was
read, providing for a basic persistence mechanism for parameter
settings across runs.

For the initial impetus to change this, see the section 'History' below.


What it does
------------

The Python API replaces access to interpreter-internal parameters by
calls to to Python methods. This way, storage and retrieval of
numbered parameters becomes fully configurable at the Python level.


How to configure the Python API:
--------------------------------

1. If you do not explicitly activate the Python API, everything
remains as before. i.e. the emc.var file is used.

2. To enable the API, configure 
    [RS274NGC]
    PYTHON_API=1
in your ini file.

3. Any numbered parameter access by the interpreter will cause a
Python method to be called to set, or get a parameter value, and to
save parameters persistently, as well as restoring params from
permanent storage. The API is outlined below.

4. If enabled, the Python params access methods must be available to
the interpreter. To do so, the following is needed:

The embedded Python code needs a toplevel.py file and it needs to be
found on startup. See the Remapping documentation
(http://www.linuxcnc.org/docs/devel/html/remap/structure.html#_configuring_embedded_python_a_id_remap_embedded_python_a)
how to configure the Python plugin; a running example is contained in
this commit.

4a: in your ini file, specify the toplevel Python file, and where it can be found:

[PYTHON]
TOPLEVEL=toplevel.py
PATH_APPEND=.

4b: the toplevel.py file needs to import the params module:

--- toplevel.py ---
import params
--- end of toplevel.py ---

4c: the params.py contains the actual definition of the Python
methods, the method signatures are:

--- params.py ---
def __init__(self):  # called once during startup
    return INTERP_OK    

def set_param(self, index, newvalue, notify):
    return INTERP_OK    

def get_param(self, index):
    return INTERP_OK    

def save_params(self):
    return INTERP_OK    

def restore_params(self, *args):
    return INTERP_OK    
--- end of params.py ---


A complete working  example
---------------------------

A working example of ini file and Python files can be found in configs/sim/axis:

ini file: configs/sim/axis/axis_mm_python_api.ini

configs/sim/axis/toplevel.py: as above

configs/sim/axis/params.py: this now connects to the redis server to
implement parameter handling as in the previous Redis/C-based branch
redis-params. However, you are free to use any other storage mechanism
you see fit.


Trying it out:
---------------

Just run 'linuxcnc axis_mm_python_api.ini'. To trace the Python API,
set in the ini

   [EMC]DEBUG = 0x00080000   # trace Python API

This flag is currently only consulted in the embedded Python code, see
configs/sim/axis/params.py. To see the trace output, start linuxcnc
from a terminal window.


Notification of parameter changes in the 'UI range' (#5610 - #5710)
---------------------------------------------------

The Redis publish/subscribe mechanism is in place as before to obtain
update notifications of changes in the 'UI parameter range' . To trace
what is going on:

- start 'linuxcnc  axis_mm_python_api.ini' as above
- in a separate window, 
  $ cd to src/redis
  $ python subscribe-test.py
- in the linuxcnc MDI window, now change a 'UI parameter', say '#5610 = 4711'
- the Python script should react showing what was changed.


Other uses of the Python API, eg. for Tool geometry access:
-----------------------------------------------------------

To add other embedded Python calls, go about it like so:

- define a Python namesapce. For instance, the tool-related methods might live in
  the 'toolstore.py' module. Import that module from toplevel.py as per above.
- identify the spots in the interpreter code where properties are read and set.
- define an C++ interpreter method to deal with get/set; take
  interp_params.cc:get/set_numbered_parameter as an example.
- the latter shows how to conditionally call out into Python to achieve a task.
- the Interp::call_method API has been developed to make this very simple,
  see the comments in interp_python.cc about usage.
- in the Python method, you can access just about any Interpreter-internal data structure;
  however, this is largely undocumented - have a look at interpmodule.cc for details.
- create the Python methods in toolstore.py
- try running the new methods interactively, for instance 'rs274 -i <inifile>'
  where the inifile enables the Python API and assures proper imports.



History
-------

This came about by Daniel Rogge's patch for 'Python gui-land access to
set and get emc.var parameters', see
http://sourceforge.net/tracker/?func=detail&aid=3558022&group_id=6744&atid=356744

The subsequent discussion agreed on reworking this into using Redis
for the first time to store, exchange and notifiy users about
parameter changes. As a consequence, and due to the lack of up-to-date
packages for Lucid, the redis, redis-py and hiredis code was
integrated into master as a temporary measure
(http://git.linuxcnc.org/gitweb?p=linuxcnc.git;a=commit;h=ef451a436719deeb97d321ca63fbd34c83eea673
and subsequent diffs).

Based on this, I did a first-round implementation of replacing access
to emc.var file by C-based Redis code:
http://git.mah.priv.at/gitweb/emc2-dev.git/shortlog/refs/heads/redis-params

While this works fine, it requires quite a bit of supporting C code,
and the major disadvantage is that any Redis data model now is
'compiled in' into the interpreter. This branch was reviewed, but never merged.

A recent discussion about 'The Ideal tooltable' again brought up
moving tool parameters to Redis, and at that point I decided it would
be better to drop the idea of accessing Redis directly in C, and go
through external Python stubs for such tasks as setting/getting
parameters or getting/setting tool geometry.

This branch implements this C->Python callout API, and is intended to
serve as a model how the tool API can be done. The major difference is
that there are no references to Redis in C/C++ code anymore, and hence
no compiled-in datamodel.

This means that any storage or database mechanism available in Python
can be used to store and retrieve numbered parameters, and eventually
tool information, and the actual storage mechanism becomes amenable to
integrator adaptation, which was not the case before.



